ding set is what `bash tools/check-rule-routes.sh --diff d3b92562fa61795f007a6167f89bb22412a25237`, run inside    `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.cfer8J/cases/0/tree/case.JtWwLe/repo/.pi/worktrees/loop`, derives for the paths the diff touched** — the guideline sections and the named rules    that own those paths. Read each, and grade the change per rule name. A section the change's artifacts name    beyond that set is an **addition** to the reading set: record it as an addition, never as a substitution    for a derived section. A derived section the change does not mention is graded anyway, because the map,    not the change, is what the review reads. 8. **New overreach** — every claim no evidence supports, verbatim as `UNPROVEN: …`.  ## Grade every finding  Prefix every finding you raise with one of two words, at the start of its own line:  ``` BLOCKING: <the finding> NOTE: <the finding> ```  **`BLOCKING:`** — the change cannot be merged as it stands: the contract is contradicted, a scenario is not exercised by the case that names it, or the gate or the red-case evidence is missing or false. **`NOTE:`** — everything the change does not depend on: wording, a pointer, a naming, an ordering, a restatement, a nicer way to say the same thing, and a quoted number whose command is not in the record. A note is recorded on the item and costs the change no round.  A finding you cannot grade is **blocking**: an ungraded refusal is read as a refusal.  **When the item is a falsification.** A round can reach you with an empty diff, because the worker answered that the item's premise does not hold (`LOOP-FALSIFIED` and `LOOP-MEASURED` in its report). The diff cannot show whether that is right, so the report is the whole claim and it is read like one: does the measurement cover the *mechanism* the item blames, and can it be repeated from what the worker named? A measurement of something else, a number with no origin, or a claimed falsification that left files 